ARM Condensed Reference File (CRF) format
=========================================

The CRF file is used to encode test patterns for chips; it makes
full use of the strobe capabilities of a tester.

The CRF file takes the form of a table of stimuli - each line in the
table represents a tester cycle which shows what stimuli should be
applied to the chip, and what results are expected on the outputs. The
strobe timing information is given in a separate file (the "CTRM"
file), and its format is described later.

Each pin of the chip has an associated column in the CRF table, and
various symbols are used to represent the signal level and direction
of drive (into or out of the chip) for that pin in each tester cycle:-

CRF file test characters
========================
                          +------------------------------+
                          |       SIGNAL  LEVEL          |
                          +-------+--------+-------------+
                          |  LOW  |  HIGH  |  UNDEFINED  |
   +-----------+----------+-------+--------+-------------+
   |           |    INPUT |  "L"  |  "H"   |    "u"      |
   |   SIGNAL  +----------+-------+--------+-------------+
   | DIRECTION |   OUTPUT |  "0"  |  "1"   |    "."      |
   |           +----------+-------+--------+-------------+
   |           | UNDRIVEN |  "s"  |  "t"   |    "z"      |
   +-----------+----------+-------+--------+-------------+

    INPUT - signifies that the signal is being driven INTO the chip under
            test.

   OUTPUT - signifies that the signal is being driven OUT OF the chip
            under test.

 UNDRIVEN - signifies that the signal WAS being driven OUT OF the chip
            under test, but that it is no longer being driven.

The CTRM file is an adjunct to the CRF file which describes the type
of strobe to be used for each pin on the chip. There are three types
of input strobe (RT0, RT1 & NRZ) and one type of output strobe
(CHK). These strobes fully define the timing associated with each pin
for the entire CRF test (i.e. there are no dynamic strobe changes
during the test).

CTRM file strobes
=================

RT0(t1,t2)
~~~~~~~~~~

Return-to-zero strobe; if an "H" character is specified in a CRF
vector, the corresponding pin should be driven high at time 't1,' and
low again at time 't2.' If an "L" or "u" character is specified, the
pin should be driven low for the cycle duration.

RT1(t1,t2)
~~~~~~~~~~

Return-to-one strobe; if an "L" or "u" character is specified in a CRF
vector, the corresponding pin should be driven low at time 't1,' and
high again at time 't2.' If an "H" character is specified, the pin
should be driven high for the cycle duration.

NRZ(t1)
~~~~~~~

Non-return-to-zero strobe; if an "L" or "u" character is specified in
a CRF vector, the corresponding pin should be driven low at time 't1.'
If an "H" character is specified, the pin should be driven high at
time 't1.'

CHK(t1)
~~~~~~~

Check strobe; if any of the "0", "1", ".", "s", "t" or "z" characters
are specified in a CRF vector, the tester must *not* drive the chip
for the duration of the cycle. If either the "0" or "1" characters
were specified, the tester should sample the value on the pin at time
't1' and flag any difference between the sampled & specified output
level. Note that the "." character implies that the pin output level
should be ignored for that cycle.  The characters "s", "t" and "z"
mean that the tester should check that the pin is not being driven by
the chip.

In order to run the CRF test patterns, each vector should be
considered as a test cycle:-

1) Any pin of the chip which is marked as an INPUT ("L", "H" or "u")
in the table should be interpreted as a signal to be driven into the
chip by the tester during that cycle. Note that the character "u"
should be treated as "L" (i.e. a low level input); the "u" character
should only occur in the first few vectors of the test if at all.

Input strobes may be specified as RT0, RT1 or NRZ, but bidirectional
pins are only ever attached to NRZ strobes in the CRF tests supplied.

In the case of bidirectional signals, it is important to avoid drive
clashes between the tester and chip. It is assumed that the tester
enables its drivers at the start of a test cycle (around 0ns) if the
pin is marked as an INPUT for that vector, but that the value
initially input by the tester matches the value output by the chip in
the preceding (output) cycle. The tester then switches to the new
input level at the specified NRZ strobe point in the cycle.

2) Any pin that is marked as an OUTPUT ("0", "1" or ".") in the table
indicates that the chip will be driving the appropriate value out of
the pin during that cycle; the tester must disable its drivers at the
start of the cycle (around 0ns) in order to avoid a drive clash.

The level on the pin should be sampled at the time specified by the
CHK strobe, and compared to the value predicted in the CRF file; the
character "0" indicates that the chip is expected to be driving the
pin low at the sample time, "1" indicates a high level drive, and "."
indicates an undefined drive level (which the tester should treat as
don't care).

3) Any pin that is marked as UNDRIVEN ("s", "t" or "z") in the table
indicates that the chip should stop driving the pin during that cycle;
the tester must *not* drive the pin during such cycles, and should
check that the chip has stopped driving the pin.

CTRM file format
================
 The CTRM file includes information on both the test strobes to be used in
conjunction with the CRF file, and the sample points that were used in the
original simulation to generate the test.
 An example file is shown below:

     ! ##################
     ! # ARM60 AC2 Test #
     ! ##################
     !
     ! Note: All propagation & strobe times doubled to guarantee simulation
     !       Hold times adjusted suitably.


     CYCLE: Xmclk (/)  PRD(200)

     INPUTS:
     Xmclk      <I> Xmclk (/)  RT0(30,130)
     XNwait     <I> Xmclk (/)  RT0(20,140)

     Xbigend    <I> Xmclk (\)  NRZ(100)
     Xlateabt   <I> Xmclk (\)  NRZ(100)
     Xprog32    <I> Xmclk (\)  NRZ(100)
     Xdata32    <I> Xmclk (\)  NRZ(100)

     XNreset    <I> Xmclk (/)  NRZ(110)
     XNirq      <I> Xmclk (\)  NRZ(110)
     XNfiq      <I> Xmclk (\)  NRZ(110)
     Xabort     <I> Xmclk (/)  NRZ(50)
     Xcpa       <I> Xmclk (/)  NRZ(50)
     Xcpb       <I> Xmclk (/)  NRZ(50)
     Xale       <I> Xmclk (/)  RT0(32,130)
     Xabe       <I> Xmclk (/)  RT0(31,130)
     Xdbe       <I> Xmclk (/)  RT0(30,140)

     XNTrst     <I> Xmclk (/)  NRZ(110)
     XTck       <I> Xmclk (/)  NRZ(110)
     XTms       <I> Xmclk (/)  NRZ(110)
     XTdi       <I> Xmclk (/)  NRZ(110)

     BIDIRECTIONAL:
     Xd[31:0]   <I> Xmclk (\) <O> Xmclk (\) <D> Xmclk (\)  NRZ(110) CHK(131)

     OUTPUTS:
     XTdo       <O> Xmclk (/)  CHK(131)

     Xa[31:0]   <O> Xmclk (\)  CHK(90)
     XNmreq     <O> Xmclk (\)  CHK(131)
     Xseq       <O> Xmclk (\)  CHK(131)
     XNrw       <O> Xmclk (\)  CHK(110)
     XNbw       <O> Xmclk (\)  CHK(110)
     Xlock      <O> Xmclk (\)  CHK(110)
     XNtrans    <O> Xmclk (\)  CHK(110)
     XNopc      <O> Xmclk (\)  CHK(80)
     XNcpi      <O> Xmclk (\)  CHK(131)

     END

 With reference to the example above:

 1) The "CYCLE" statement defines the period of the tester cycle.
     e.g.
       PRD(200) ==> 200ns tester cycle

    When the patterns are extracted, the signal specified defines when
    a new pattern is made.  eg in the above example, a new vector is
    made on every RISING edge of Xmclk.

 2) The "INPUTS:" keyword denotes that the following pins are all input
    only. For each pin, the tester strobe information is shown at the end of
    the line.
    e.g.
      XNwait     <I> Xmclk (/)  RT0(20,140)
      ======     =============  ===========
        |              |             |
      Signal        Ignore         Test input
       name   (sample point info)    strobe

    The sample point info defines when the signal is sampled as the
    patterns are made.  In this case, the signal is sampled on the
    RISING edge of Xmclk, and the value becomes the value for that
    vector.

 3) The "BIDIRECTIONAL:" keyword denotes that the following pins are all
    bidirectionals. For each pin, both an input strobe time and output
    check time are specified at the end of the line.
    e.g.
      Xd[31:0]  <I> Xmclk (\) <O> Xmclk (\) <D> Xmclk (\) NRZ(110) CHK(131)
      ========  ========================================= ======== ========
         |                        |                          |        |
      Signal                   Ignore                      Test     Test
       name              (sample point info)               input    output
     (a bus in                                             strobe   strobe
      this case)

    The sample point info defines when the signal is sampled as the
    vectors are made.  In this case, when Xd[] is an input bus, the
    value of the vector is the value on the bus at the FALLING edge of
    Xmclk.  When the vectors are played back, this new value is
    applied 110ns into the cycle.  When the bus is configured as
    output, the value in the vectors is the value on the FALLING edge
    of Xmclk.  Note that the input and output sample edge can be
    different.  The direction of the data bus for the vector is
    determined at the sample time specified after the <D>.  In this
    case, the direction of the bus is determined on the FALLING edge
    of Xmclk.

 4) The "OUTPUTS:" keyword denotes that the following pins are all output
    only. For each pin, the tester strobe information is shown at the end of
    the line.
    e.g.
      Xa[31:0]   <O> Xmclk (\)  CHK(90)
      ======     =============  =======
        |              |            |
      Signal        Ignore        Test output
       name   (sample point info)   strobe

    The sample point info defines when the output is sampled as the
    vectors are made.  In this case, the value for the vector is the
    value sampled on the falling edge of Xmclk.

  5) The "END" keyword denotes the end of the file.



Other notes
===========
 1) The number quoted at the end of each vector (e.g. "}  12030") represents
the simulation time at which the vector was generated, and makes a useful
reference which is independent of the number of vectors in a file.

2) Comments may be included in the table at appropriate points - they take the
form of a hash in the first column followed by the comment text.
 e.g.
       "# Comment line"

3) No outputs should be checked until the specified vector is reached (to
allow for chip initialisation). The vector at which the outputs should be
checked may be marked with the comment "# $check on". All outputs from the
start of the test up to the specified vector should be treated as don't
care.

4) The comment "# $test end" denotes the end of the test, and no further
vectors should be checked. If this comment is omitted, the end of the table
denotes the end of the test.

Sample Point Info Notes
=======================

In the above example, the period of the vectors was defined from Xmclk
rising to Xmclk rising.

             _________           _____
        ____/         \_________/

            |<- Vector Period ->|

When a signal is defined to be sampled on the rising edge of this
clock, the sample occurs on the rising edge at the END of the cycle.
Likewise, for a bidirectional bus where the direction test is
performed on the rising edge, this happens at the end of the cycle.

Sampling HighZ Values in CRF
============================

When a high impedance signal is sampled, either an "s" or a "t" will
be written into the vector file.  An "s" denotes that a signal went
from logic "0" to high-impedance, a "t" denotes that a signal went
from logic "1" to high impedance.  However, the vector written into
the CRF file does not necessarily have the correct value.  The
sampling mechanism is correct in that is notices that the signal has
gone high impedance, but always takes the value from the previous
vector, not the current value.  Consider the following example where
the 3-State output signal AOUT, with enable EN, is sampled on the
falling edge of CLK :

                ________          ________
      CLK _____/        \________/        \_______
          __________________________
      EN                            \____________
             _______________
     AOUT __/               \__________ZZZZZZZZZZ


Here, since AOUT was low and then went high-impedance, the correct
value to appear in the output vectors would be "s".  However, when the
CRF sampler sees that a signal has gone high-impedance, it bases the
"s" or "t" value on the previously sampled value.  Thus, in the above
case, the value "t" gets written into the test patterns.

Although this behaviour is incorrect, it does not actually matter too
much.  The purpose of the above test is to drive AOUT from logic "0"
to high-impedance (and later in the test the signal should go from
logic "1" to high-impedance).  When the patterns are run on the
tester, this device will exhibit the desired behaviour.  The tester
will interpret both "s" and "t" as high-impedance, and should ignore
the value since there is no way for the tester to tell whether the
signal was a "1" or a "0" before going tristate.  Therefore, tester
will simply check that AOUT has gone to the high-impedance state, and
the test will work as intended.

                            ==============


